{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Downward-bias correction: forecasts are systematically too high by ~0.2–1.5 EUR in the first ~1–10 days; apply a small negative offset to the model path early-horizon (larger when the model level is >~69).",
    "Trend/level lag after regime shift: when the market breaks higher (late-horizon in examples), the model underestimates the upswing (often by ~2–6 EUR by days ~25–30). Add a positive drift/tilt to the back end when recent history shows rising momentum (e.g., last 5–10 days slope > 0).",
    "Excess mean-reversion in the mid-horizon: model tends to pull prices back toward ~67–69 too aggressively, missing sustained trends. Reduce mean-reversion strength when realized volatility and/or trend persistence is elevated.",
    "Spike handling asymmetry: after upward spikes, the model often overshoots slightly in the next few days, but then fails to follow through on continued strength later. Use a two-stage adjustment: dampen immediate post-spike overshoot (days 1–5), but increase tail responsiveness (days 20–30) if the spike is confirmed by subsequent higher closes.",
    "Horizon-dependent calibration: apply piecewise correction—(i) days 1–10: subtract small bias; (ii) days 11–20: minimal adjustment; (iii) days 21–30: add momentum-based uplift proportional to recent trend and distance from recent highs."
  ]
}
